A||||15||JohnnyK||Increasing HP on a briggs motor? :)||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||12-09-2000||11:34 AM||||As you may know, I'm trying to build a go kart, but the only horizontal shaft motor I have at this time is an old tired 3.5 HP one. My question is this: there MUST be an easy way to get an extra 1-2 HP out of those things (without rebuilding). Some things like alternate fuel? I have model rc fuel with 5-10% nitro in it. What would happen if I ran that? Something interesting I assume.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||82-T/A||12-09-2000||12:20 PM||||Dude, when you find out, let me know.<P>My dad just bought a new Riding Mower for their new house. It's got a 12.5hp Briggs and Straton motor. <P>I'd like to get some more power out of it.. for the times when I go over to their house to mow the lawn. Maybe I can race a few Hondas with it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||12-09-2000||12:40 PM||||alright, if it is an older motor that is a flathead try milling or sanding the head down for more compression.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||12-09-2000||01:42 PM||||Sanding the head down? Inside the cylinder? Wouldn't that give me less compression?||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||12-09-2000||02:06 PM||||When my brother was a kid he had rebuilt a lawnmower engine.He set it up on our front lawn and ran it on model airplane fuel.It ran so fast you could only hear a pop from the exhaust once in a while.When it shut off it was one piece of metal(it had melted everthing that could melt)||||reg||1
